>
  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    "Should I buy or keep renting?" is one of the biggest financial decisions most people ever
    make — often a difference of hundreds of thousands over a few decades. Yet the tools available
    are surprisingly poor. The best-known calculator sits behind a newspaper paywall. Many free
    ones are built by parties with a stake in your answer — lenders, brokerages, listing sites —
    and most quietly skip the factors that decide the outcome: the opportunity cost of your down
    payment, selling costs, tax rules as they actually work, and what the cheaper party should be
    doing with the monthly difference.
  </p>
  <p>
    RentVsBuyMath.com is our answer: a genuinely free calculator that counts everything, explains
    everything, and doesn't care which answer you get.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes it different</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Symmetric math.</strong> Whichever path is cheaper in a given year, that saver
      invests the difference. Most calculators only credit the renter — which quietly biases the
      result.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everything on the table.</strong> Mortgage amortization, PMI with automatic
      cancellation, closing and selling costs, property tax growth, maintenance, insurance, rent
      inflation, investment fees, capital gains tax, and the U.S. home-sale exclusion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Documented formulas.</strong> The full model is public on the
      <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a> — every formula, every default, every source. If
      you find an error, tell us and we'll fix it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Honest about uncertainty.</strong> The verdict comes with a sensitivity check
      showing which assumptions could flip it.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No paywall, no signup, no data collection.</strong> Calculations run in your browser.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
  <p>
    RentVsBuyMath was built by <strong>Jonathan Nyst</strong>, a fintech growth professional, as an
    independent project. It has no affiliation with any lender, brokerage, or real-estate platform.
    The site is supported by advertising, which never influences the math: the calculator's output
    depends only on your inputs.
  </p>

  <h2>Open methodology, open source</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation engine is open source under the MIT license and covered by an automated test
    suite. Corrections and suggestions are welcome — reach us via the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
